\title{The rank lemma}
\taxon{Lemma}
\date{2024-01-15}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{Let #{A \subseteq \mathbb{F}_3^n}, and suppose #{P \in V_n^d} is such that #{P(a + a') = 0} for all #{a \neq a' \in A}. Then}

##{|\lbrace a \in A : P(2a) \neq 0\rbrace | \leq 2m_{d/2}.}

\proof{
\p{Every #{P \in V_n^d} can be written as a linear combination of monomials from #{M_n^d}, so}

##{P(x + y) = \sum_{\substack{m, m' \in M_n^d \\ \deg(m \cdot m') \leq d}} c_{m,m'}m(x)m'(y)}

\p{for some coefficients #{c_{m,m'}}. Since at least one of #{m, m'} has to have degree at most #{d/2}, we can write}

##{P(x + y) = \sum_{m \in M_n^{d/2}} m(x)F_m(y) + \sum_{m' \in M_n^{d/2}} m'(y)G_{m'}(x),}

\p{where #{(F_m)_{m \in M_n^{d/2}}} and #{(G_{m'})_{m' \in M_n^{d/2}}} are polynomials. Viewing #{(P(x + y))_{x,y \in A}} as an #{|A| \times |A|} matrix #{C}, we see that #{C} can be written as a sum of at most #{2m_{d/2}} matrices of rank at most #{1}. Hence #{\operatorname{rank}(C) \leq 2m_{d/2}}. But #{C} is a diagonal matrix by assumption, whose rank equals #{|\lbrace a \in A : P(2a) \neq 0\rbrace |}.}
}
